        "step_anchor": "step-1",
        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the <strong>product rule</strong> for logarithms.</p>",
            "readable_text": "We can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the product rule for logarithms.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The <strong>product rule</strong> states that two logarithms which share a common base <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\color{blue}n]</span> satisfy\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "The product rule states that two logarithms which share a common base [MATH: \\color{blue}n] satisfy\n[MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>So, we can use the product rule to rewrite <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)]</span> as</p>",
            "readable_text": "So, we can use the product rule to rewrite [MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)] as",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log_{5} 4 + \\log_{5} (3)|= \\log_{5} (4 \u22c5 3); = \\log_{5} (12).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\log_{5} 4 + \\log_{5} (3)|= \\log_{5} (4 \u22c5 3); = \\log_{5} (12).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms.</p>",
            "readable_text": "The product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nWe can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the product rule for logarithms.\n\nContent:\nThe product rule states that two logarithms which share a common base [MATH: \\color{blue}n] satisfy\n[MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).]\n\nContent:\nSo, we can use the product rule to rewrite [MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)] as\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\log_{5} 4 + \\log_{5} (3)|= \\log_{5} (4 \u22c5 3); = \\log_{5} (12).]\n\nContent:\nThe product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms."
      },
