          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>First, let&#x27;s recall the product rule for logarithms:</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)]</span></p><p>If we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get</p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3\\log_{5} 7 + 3\\log_{5} 3|= 3(\\log_{5} 7 + \\log_{5} 3); = 3\\log_{5} (7 \u22c5 3); = 3\\log_{5} 21.]</span></p><p>Therefore,\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n[MATH: \\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)]\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n[MATH: 3\\log_{5} 7 + 3\\log_{5} 3|= 3(\\log_{5} 7 + \\log_{5} 3); = 3\\log_{5} (7 \u22c5 3); = 3\\log_{5} 21.]\n\nTherefore,\n[MATH: 3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .]",
