    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the product rule for logarithms.\n\nContent:\nThe product rule states that two logarithms which share a common base [MATH: \\color{blue}n] satisfy\n[MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).]\n\nContent:\nSo, we can use the product rule to rewrite [MATH: \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)] as\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\log_{5} 4 + \\log_{5} (3)|= \\log_{5} (4 \u22c5 3); = \\log_{5} (12).]\n\nContent:\nThe product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Combining Logarithms Using the Product Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\log_3(2)+\\log_3(5)] as a single logarithm.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n[MATH: \\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)]\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n[MATH: \\log_{3} 2 + \\log_{3} 5|= \\log_{3} (2 \u22c5 5); = \\log_{3} 10.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nWrite the following expression as a single logarithm with base [MATH: 6.][MATH: \\log_{6} 3 + \\log_{6} 7 =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\log_{5} 2 + \\log_{5} 4 + \\log_{5} 6] as a single logarithm.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\log_{15} 12]\nb. [MATH: \\log_{5} 48]\nc. [MATH: \\log_{15} 48]\nd. [MATH: \\log_{5} 10]\ne. [MATH: \\log_{125} 48]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Combining Logarithms With Coefficients Using the Product Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: 3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3}] as a single logarithm.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n[MATH: \\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)]\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n[MATH: 3\\log_{5} 7 + 3\\log_{5} 3|= 3(\\log_{5} 7 + \\log_{5} 3); = 3\\log_{5} (7 \u22c5 3); = 3\\log_{5} 21.]\n\nTherefore,\n[MATH: 3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nExpress [MATH: 2\\log_{4} 8 + 2\\log_{4} 3] as a single logarithm.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4\\log_{16} 24]\nb. [MATH: \\log_{4} 48]\nc. [MATH: 2\\log_{8} 22]\nd. [MATH: 2\\log_{4} 24]\ne. [MATH: 2\\log_{4} 11]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nExpress [MATH: 2\\log_{3} 4 + 2\\log_{3} 5 + 2\\log_{3} 2] as a single logarithm.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 8\\log_{3} 40]\nb. [MATH: 6\\log_{3} 40]\nc. [MATH: \\log_{3} 80]\nd. [MATH: 2\\log_{3} 40]\ne. [MATH: 2\\log_{27} 40]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Separating a Logarithm Into Two Logarithms Using the Product Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\ln\\left(x^2 y\\right)] as a sum of two logarithms.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the product rule for natural logarithms:\n\n[MATH: \\ln{a} + \\ln{b} = \\ln(ab)]\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n[MATH: \\ln (x^{2}y)|= \\ln (x^{2} \u22c5 y); = \\ln (x^{2}) + \\ln y.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \\ln (4p^{3}q) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \\ln (p^{3}) + \\ln (q^{4})]\nb. [MATH: \\ln (p^{3}) + 4\\ln q]\nc. [MATH: \\ln 4 + \\ln (p^{3}) + \\ln q]\nd. [MATH: 4\\ln (p^{3}) + \\ln q]\ne. [MATH: 4 + \\ln (p^{3}) + \\ln q]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 4\\log_{5} (4y) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 8\\log_{5} 4 + \\log_{5} y]\nb. [MATH: \\log_{5} 16 + 4\\log_{5} y]\nc. [MATH: 4\\log_{5} 4 + 4\\log_{5} y]\nd. [MATH: 4\\log_{5} 4 + 8\\log_{5} y]\ne. [MATH: 8\\log_{5} 4 + 8\\log_{5} y]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Example: Evaluating an Expression Using the Product Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nIf [MATH: \\log_2(3)=k] and [MATH: \\log_2(x)=p,] what is the value of [MATH: \\log_2(3x)?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n[MATH: \\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)]\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n[MATH: \\log_{2} (3x)|= \\log_{2} 3 + \\log_{2} x; = k + p.]\n\n---\n\nItem 12 \u2014 Question 7\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: \\log_{2} x = p] and [MATH: \\log_{2} y = q], then [MATH: \\log_{2} (xy) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: p + q]\nb. [MATH: p \u2212 q]\nc. [MATH: (p)/(q)]\nd. [MATH: pq]\ne. [MATH: 2pq]\n\n---\n\nItem 13 \u2014 Question 8\n\nPrompt:\nIf [MATH: \\log_{z} r = 0.25] and [MATH: \\log_{z} 6 = 0.2], then [MATH: \\log_{z} (6r) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 0.20]\nb. [MATH: 0.25]\nc. [MATH: 0.45]\nd. [MATH: 0.50]\ne. [MATH: 0.05]"
